# Glucose Profile (AGP)

The Ambulatory Glucose Profile (AGP) answers the question "what does a typical
day look like?". It lays all the days of the period on top of each other, so
the patterns that repeat become clear. The AGP is a standard report format that
care teams know.

<Video id="agp" />

## What it shows

### The key figures

The strip at the top shows six figures, with the consensus target under most of
them:

| Figure | Target on the page |
|--------|--------------------|
| **Time in range** (70-180 mg/dL) | 70% or more |
| **Average** | None |
| **eHbA1c**, from your average glucose | None |
| **CV** (coefficient of variation) | 33% or less |
| **Below range** (below 70 mg/dL) | Less than 4% |
| **Above range** (above 180 mg/dL) | Less than 25% |

### Glucose Pattern (24-hour overlay)

This is the main chart. The horizontal axis is the time of day, from midnight
to midnight.

- **The median line** is the middle reading at each time of day. It shows what
  happens most often.
- **The darker band** (25th to 75th percentile) is where your glucose is for
  half of the time.
- **The lighter band** (10th to 90th percentile) is where your glucose is for
  80% of the time.
- **The shaded target band**, with the dashed **Low** and **High** lines, marks
  your target range.

Move the pointer over the chart to see the five values at that time: P90, P75,
**Median**, P25 and P10.

Where the bands are narrow, your days are alike at that time. Where the bands
are wide, your days are different from each other at that time.

### Time in Range Distribution

The bar shows how your time divides across five ranges: very low, low, in
range, high and very high. The labels next to the bar give the percentage for
each range. Move the pointer over the bar to see all the values, including the
tight range (70-140 mg/dL).

### Measured Against Consensus Targets

This card puts three figures next to their international consensus targets:

- Time in target range: at least 70%.
- Coefficient of variation (CV): 33% or below.
- Time below 70 mg/dL: under 4%.

The app shows glucose values in mmol/L if you chose mmol/L.

## How to use it

The AGP opens on the last 14 days. Two weeks can show a pattern that is not
typical. To make sure that a pattern is real, look at a longer period:

1. At the top of the report, select the date range.
2. Select **30 Days**.
3. Select **Apply Filters**. The whole report loads again for 30 days.
4. Compare the shape of the median line with the shape for 14 days.

Select **What is an AGP?** and then **How to read this chart** for a short
explanation on the page. Select **Summary** to open the
[Executive Summary](/docs/reports/executive-summary).

## Good to know

- The AGP is most reliable with readings on at least 14 days. If the period has
  too few days with readings, a badge shows how many days the report uses.
- If the chart shows **No pattern data**, the period has too few readings.
  Choose a longer period.
